Missing Content Security Policy	A Content Security Policy (CSP) header is a response header that is sent from a web server to a web browser to instruct the browser on which sources of content are allowed to be loaded and executed on the web page.
HSTS Not Set	If the Web Application response doesnot contain Strict Transport Security Header, then the website is vulnerable to attacks that exploit the use of insecure HTTP connections.
Insufficient HSTS Expiration Time	An insufficient HSTS expiration time means that the max-age directive in the HSTS header is too short, which can leave the website vulnerable to attacks that exploit the use of insecure HTTP connections
Missing XSS Protection	If the "X-XSS-Protection" header is missing from a website's server response, the browser may not have its built-in XSS protection enabled, leaving the website vulnerable to XSS attacks.
XSS Protection is disabled	Web Application response contains XSS Protection Header. However the value is set to disabled (0) instead of enabled (1)
Missing X-Frame Options	If the "X-Frame-Options" header is missing from a website's server response, it may leave the website vulnerable to clickjacking attacks.
Missing X-Content-Type Header	If the "X-Content-Type-Options" header is missing from a website's server response, it may leave the website vulnerable to content sniffing attacks.
Missing Cache Control and Pragma Headers	"The ""Cache-Control"" and ""Pragma"" headers are used to control caching of a website's resources, such as HTML pages, CSS files, and images.
If these headers are missing from a website's server response, it can lead to a lack of control over how the content is cached by browsers and intermediaries, potentially leading to issues with stale content, security vulnerabilities, or reduced website performance."
Information Disclosure in Headers	Information disclosure in headers occurs when sensitive information is unintentionally disclosed in the HTTP response headers sent by a web server. This information can include server software version,Private IP, different application Paths, etc.
Unencrypted Communication	Unencrypted communication refers to the transmission of data over a network or the internet without any form of encryption or protection. 
