Check for the leakage of the API keys to perform actions in the cloud buckets	API keys are often used to authenticate and authorize access to cloud storage buckets. If these API keys are leaked, attackers can use them to perform actions on the buckets, including reading, modifying, or deleting data. 
Check if listing is enabled in cloud buckets	To check if listing is enabled in cloud buckets, one can try to access the bucket directly or use a tool like AWS CLI or Google Cloud SDK to list the objects in the bucket. If the objects are listed, then the listing is enabled.
Check if the files can be downloaded or accessed without a token from the bucket	To check for this vulnerability, one can attempt to access files in the bucket without providing any authentication or authorization credentials. They can do this by attempting to download files or access them directly via a URL. If the files can be downloaded or accessed without credentials, then the bucket may have an access control misconfiguration vulnerability.
Check SSRF vulnerabilities 	To check for SSRF vulnerabilities, one should identify input fields that can accept URLs and possible attack vectors, such as local or internal network IP addresses.
Check for unrestricted file upload	To check for unrestricted file upload vulnerability, one can attempt to upload various types of files to the application, including PHP or ASP files, shell scripts, and executable files. 
Check for file upload bypass via double extension	To check for file upload bypass via double extension, one can attempt to upload a file with a double extension, or a file with a non-standard extension that may be treated as an executable file by the server, such as ".pl" or ".py".
Check for file upload bypass	To check for file upload bypass vulnerabilities, a tester can attempt to upload various types of files to the application, including files with different extensions or encoded content. 
Check for RCE via file upload	To check for RCE via file upload vulnerabilities, a tester can attempt to upload various types of files to the application, including malicious files with embedded code that can be executed on the server
Check for Application Level DOS via Pixel Flood Attack	To check for this vulnerability, a tester can simulate a Pixel Flood Attack by sending a large number of pixel-sized requests to the server and monitoring its response. The tester can then check if the server is able to handle the requests and maintain its functionality, or if it becomes unresponsive.
Check for file upload size restriction	To check for file upload size restriction, a tester can attempt to upload files that exceed the size limit set by the application and monitor its response
Check for XSS via images	To check for XSS via images, a tester can attempt to upload an image containing malicious code to the application, and then check if the code is executed when the image is displayed on the web page
Malicious File Upload (Applicable Only for Production Application)	To check for malicious file upload, a tester can attempt to upload a file containing malicious code to the application and then check if the code is executed when the file is accessed. 
Check for XXE/SSRF via excel files	To check for these vulnerabilities in Excel files, it's important to ensure that the application handling the files properly validates any XML data and does not allow external entity references or malicious URLs to be executed.
Check for OTP Bruteforce	OTP bruteforce is an attack where one tries to guess the correct OTP by trying out every possible combination of OTPs until the correct one is found. This attack can be conducted manually or with the help of automated tools.
Check for OTP expiration	Once an OTP is generated, it is typically only valid for a short period of time, usually a few minutes, before it expires and becomes unusable
Check for Default OTP	The use of default OTPs can compromise the security of a system or service, as these OTPs may be well known or easy to guess, allowing an attacker to gain unauthorized access to an account.
Check if the OTP is getting validated at the client side or server side	To provide the highest level of security, it's recommended to implement OTP validation on both the client-side and server-side. Client-side validation can provide a better user experience, while server-side validation can provide a more secure environment for validating the OTP.
Check OTP bypass via response manipulation	Intercept the OTP request/response using a proxy or other interception tool, and then modifies the response to return a successful validation result, even if the OTP provided by the user is incorrect or invalid
Check OTP reuse	The reuse of an OTP can compromise the security of the system and make it vulnerable to attacks. If one intercepts an OTP that has been reused, they can gain unauthorized access to the user's account 
Check for OTP leakage in request/response of the application	. If the OTP is included in the request or response of the application, it can be intercepted by an attacker who has access to the network or the user's device.
Forced Browsing to Authenticated Page/Dashboard	In a forced browsing attack, one uses a list of common URLs or parameter values to access pages.
Check for Clickjacking in all sensitive and non-sensitive pages	Check the website's HTML code to identify if any pages are using iframes. If iframe header is missing there is a possibility for clickjacking
CSRF Token Implementation	CSRF token implementation involves generating a unique token for each user session, and including this token in each request that modifies state on the server, such as a form submission.
CSRF Token Reuse	Reuse a valid CSRF token to execute unauthorized actions on behalf of an authenticated user. 
CSRF Token Removal Check	If the CSRF token is still valid after the user has logged out, this indicates a potential vulnerability 
CSRF in JSON body request	"Check if the server verifies the CSRF token and rejects the request, or if it processes the request as valid.
If the server processes the request as valid, it indicates that the application is vulnerable to CSRF attacks in JSON body requests."
Check for SQL Injection	"Identify input fields that allow the user to enter data, such as search fields, login forms, and contact forms.
Attempt to inject SQL code into these input fields, such as using a single quote or a semicolon, to see if the website produces any error messages or behaves unexpectedly."
Check for Reflected XSS	Attempt to inject a script into the input field to see if the website reflects the script back to the user.
Check for Stored XSS	Attempt to inject a script into the input field and submit the form to see if the script is stored on the server.
Check for DOM XSS	Check if the website uses client-side JavaScript to manipulate the DOM, and if user input is used in any of these manipulations without proper encoding or escaping.Attempt to inject a script into the input field and observe if the script is executed on the page.
Check for XXE	Attempt to inject an external entity into the input field to see if the website processes the external entity.
Check for CSV Injection	Attempt to inject a formula or macro into the input field and download the CSV file to see if the formula or macro is executed by the user's spreadsheet program.
Check for Command Injection	Attempt to inject a command into the input field to see if the command is executed by the server.
Host Header Injection	Test the vulnerability by attempting to manipulate the Host header of a request to see if you are able to bypass security controls or perform an attack.
SMTP Injection	Test the vulnerability by attempting to inject and execute arbitrary SMTP commands in a web application to see if you are able to bypass security controls or perform an attack.
CRLF Injection	Test the vulnerability by attempting to inject and execute arbitrary HTTP headers or other data into a web application response to see if you are able to bypass security controls or perform an attack.
LDAP Injection	"Test the vulnerability by attempting to inject and execute arbitrary LDAP queries in a web application to see if you are able to bypass security controls or perform an attack.
"
Lack of Input Validation	Test the vulnerability by attempting to inject malicious input or unexpected values into the web application to see if you are able to bypass security controls or perform an attack.
Content Spoofing	Test the vulnerability by attempting to modify or spoof the content of a web page to see if you are able to bypass security controls or perform an attack.
Server Side Template Injection (SSTI)	Test the vulnerability by attempting to inject and execute arbitrary code in server-side templates to see if you are able to bypass security controls or perform an attack.
Parameter Pollution	Test the vulnerability by submitting multiple parameters with the same name to the web application to see if you are able to bypass security controls or perform an attack.
Session Hijacking 	Test the vulnerability by attempting to steal the session identifier or cookie and use it to take control of the user's session.
Session Fixation	Test the vulnerability by attempting to set the session identifier before the user logs in, and then see if the user's session is hijacked when they do log in.
Check for Session Expiration Time	To check for session expiration time in your web application, you should review the session management settings to determine the current session expiration time. You can then test the session expiration time by logging in to the application and waiting until the session expires. Note the amount of time it takes for the session to expire
Check if HttpOnly Cookie flag is set for Session Cookie	"Use your browser's developer tools to inspect the session cookie that is set by your web application.
Look for the HttpOnly flag in the cookie settings. If the HttpOnly flag is set to true, then the session cookie is protected from XSS attacks."
Check if Secure Cookie flag is set for Session Cookie	"Use your browser's developer tools to inspect the session cookie that is set by your web application.
Look for the Secure flag in the cookie settings. If the Secure flag is set to true, then the session cookie is protected from network eavesdropping attacks."
Browser Refresh Attack	Try refreshing the web page while interacting with the application and see if any unexpected behavior occurs. For example, if there is a form that can be submitted, try refreshing the page after submitting the form and see if it gets submitted again.
Concurrent Login	Try logging in to the same user account from multiple devices or sessions simultaneously and see if the application allows it. If concurrent logins are allowed, then this may be a security issue.
Check for Static Session Token Generation	 Look for any settings that may allow for static session token generation. For example, check if the application generates session tokens using predictable patterns or algorithms.
Check for JWT Expiration Time	Attempt to reuse a JWT after its expiration time has passed. If the application accepts the expired JWT, then there may be a security issue.
Check for sensitive details in JWT	Verify that sensitive information is not included in the payload of the JWT. The payload should only include information that is necessary for authentication and authorization purposes.
JWT token manipulation/modification	 Attempt to tamper with a JWT to change its contents or to change its signature. If the application accepts the tampered token, then there may be a security issue.
JWT Null Algorithm Check	Attempt to use a null algorithm to sign a JWT. If the application accepts the JWT with a null algorithm, then there may be a security issue.
JWT Misonfiguration	Attempt to exploit any misconfigured or insecure JWT settings. For example, try to modify the claims or expiration time to gain unauthorized access to protected resources.
Check for cross domain script inclusion	Attempt to load external resources from untrusted domains to see if the web application accepts and executes the code. If the application accepts and executes the code, then there may be a CDSI vulnerability.
Check for Email Address Disclosures	Verify that the email addresses are being handled securely, such as through the use of encryption or hashing to protect them from unauthorized access.
Check for User Enumeartion	Attempt to register with a username or email address that is known to be invalid. Then, attempt to register with a username or email address that is known to be valid. Compare the error messages displayed by the application to determine if user enumeration is possible.
Check for Private IP Disclosure	Review the code of the web application to identify any instances where private IP addresses are being collected, stored, or transmitted. Attempt to access private IP addresses by manipulating parameters or other inputs to the application.
Check for Internal Path Disclosures	Review the code of the web application to identify any instances where file system paths are being collected, stored, or transmitted.  Attempt to access internal file system paths by manipulating parameters or other inputs to the application.
Check for Server Error Pages leaking details	Test for error pages, such as by intentionally triggering a 404 or 500 error and seeing what information is displayed in the resulting error page
Check for default server pages	Identify default server pages by visiting common URLs for default pages, such as /index.html, /index.php, /default.aspx, or similar. Test for default pages by attempting to access them directly or by manipulating parameters or other inputs to the application.
Check for Directoy Listing	 Identify directories that are vulnerable to directory listing by manually browsing the application and looking for directories that do not have an index file. Test for directory listing by accessing the directory URL directly or by manipulating parameters or other inputs to the application.
Check Web Archives for Information Disclosures	Search for the website in popular web archives, such as the Wayback Machine or Google Cache, to see if any snapshots of the website are available.
Check for sensitive information in query strings	Review the parameters passed in the query string for sensitive information such as passwords or session IDs. 
Robots.txt file leaking sensitive pages	Locate the robots.txt file at the root directory of a website, e.g., example.com/robots.txt and review the directives contained within it.
Check for Backup files	Common backup file extensions include .bak, .old, .zip, .tar, and .gz. Use search engines or tools such as "dirb" or "dirbuster" to scan the web server for backup files with the identified extensions.
Enumerate directories across multiple endpoints	Enumerating directories across multiple endpoints can help identify hidden directories or files that are not linked to or easily accessible from the main website or web application. To perform directory enumeration across multiple endpoints, you can use tools such as "dirb" or "dirsearch"
Fuzzing by providing invalid, unexpected and random data inputs	 Identify the input fields in the target application or system that are susceptible to fuzzing. These could be any text input fields, drop-down menus, checkboxes, or any other form elements that accept user input.
Lack of Masking Sensitive Details	 Identify the types of sensitive information that are present in the application, such as social security numbers, credit card numbers, and email addresses.
Lack of Credentials/Sensitive details obfuscation in request	Perform manual testing by intercepting and modifying requests to see if sensitive information is sent in plain text.
Browser Storing Creds in Plain Text	"Open the web browser and navigate to the settings.
Look for the section that manages passwords or logins.
Check to see if the passwords are stored in plain text or in an encrypted format.
If they are stored in plain text, this is a vulnerability."
Autocomplete Enabled in Sensitive Fields	"Check the ""autocomplete"" attribute for each field.
If the ""autocomplete"" attribute is enabled, this means that the browser can remember and suggest values for that field, which is a security vulnerability."
Arbitrary Host Header Trusted	"Send a request to the application with a modified ""Host"" header value, such as a malicious domain name.
If the application accepts the modified ""Host"" header value and returns a response, it is vulnerable to a host header attack."
No Account Lockout Policy	"Attempt to log in to the application with an incorrect password several times in quick succession.
If the application does not lock out the account after a certain number of failed attempts, it may not have an account lockout policy in place."
Apache Byterange DOS/ Slowloris DOS	To check for this vulnerability, you can use a tool such as "slowhttptest" or "slowloris" to send a large number of HTTP requests with incomplete headers to the server and monitor its response. If the server's response time slows down or it becomes unresponsive, it may be vulnerable to the Slowloris DOS attack.
Static Encryption	"Check if the same encryption key is being used for all instances of the application, or if the key is hardcoded in the code.
Check if the encryption key is being stored in a secure location or if it is accessible to unauthorized users."
Viewstate Encryption (If Applicable)	Intercept the ViewState parameter using a proxy tool such as Burp Suite, and then decode the ViewState content using an online decoder or tool like ViewState Decoder. If the ViewState is encrypted, it should be unreadable or garbled when decoded. If it is not encrypted, the content of the ViewState can be read easily.
Unprotected Files	Checking for unprotected files is an essential part of web application security testing. It involves looking for files and directories that are not protected by appropriate access controls and can be accessed without proper authentication or authorization.
GET Based Open Redirection (URL Based)	GET-based open redirection is a common vulnerability where one can modify the URL parameters to redirect the victim to a malicious website.
POST Based Open Redirection (Post Request Body)	"Identify all POST requests on the web application.
Modify the POST request body and replace the original URL with a malicious URL.
Submit the modified POST request and check if the web application redirects to the malicious URL."
Host Header Based Redirection	Look for the Host header in the request headers. The Host header should contain the domain name of the website you are accessing. Check if the server responds with a redirection status code (3xx) and a new location. The new location should be based on the value of the Host header.
Additional Headers in the request	Check if there are any additional headers in the request that were not present in the original request. If any suspicious additional headers are found , try modifying them to see if you can redirect to a different URL.
Stored Host Header Redirection	To check for this vulnerability, you can trigger the action that is expected to lead to a redirection response and check the value of the Host header in the request headers. If the Host header value matches the value in the Location header of the response, then the website may be vulnerable to Stored Host Header Redirection
Check for WordPress Version Disclosure	In the page source, look for the meta tag with the name "generator". This tag contains the version number of the WordPress site.
Check Vulnerable WordPress Versions	Look for the vulnerabilitites in search engines for the wordpress version 
Check Vulnerable WordPress Libraries and Plugins Versions	Check if any plugins or themes that are installed on the WordPress site have known security vulnerabilities.
Accessible XMLRPC.php file	Append "/xmlrpc.php" to the end of the domain name in the address bar (e.g., http://www.example.com/xmlrpc.php).  If  a message says "XML-RPC server accepts POST requests only. ", it means that the XML-RPC interface is accessible.
Check for user enumeration	Check the error message that appears on the page. If the error message says "Invalid username or password", it means that user enumeration is not possible on your site. However, if the error message says "Invalid password", it means that user enumeration is possible on your site.
Check for wordpress admin login panel	Check if the login panel is accessible or not. If you see a 404 error or a message that says "Page not found", then the login panel is not accessible. However, if you see a WordPress login page asking for a username and password, then the login panel is accessible.
Check for registration/signup in admin login panel	Check if there is a "Register" or "Signup" link on the login panel. If there is no such link, then user registration is disabled. However, if there is a link, then user registration is enabled.
Check for Content Spoofing in Login Pages	"Check the content of the login page to ensure that it is consistent with your site's branding and design.
Attempt to enter incorrect login credentials and observe the error message that appears. If the error message is different from what you would expect or contains suspicious links or instructions, it may be a sign of content spoofing."
Check for Unprotected Wordpress Directories	Append common directory names to the URL such as /wp-content/, /wp-includes/, and /wp-admin/. If any directories are unprotected, you will be able to access them without a password.
	 If any external links are broken, check if they have been hijacked. If you find a hijacked link, verify the redirect by clicking on the link and checking where it takes you. If it redirects to a malicious website, it indicates a security vulnerability that needs to be addressed.
Test for feature misuse	Test the feature by attempting to use it in ways that are not intended or documented. This can include using invalid inputs, entering too much data, or using the feature in an unexpected sequence.
Test for lack of non-repudiation 	Test the business logic by attempting to perform actions that should be logged or audited, such as placing an order or making a payment. Then, check to see if the system logs the actions in a way that provides non-repudiation
Test for integrity of data	Test the business logic by attempting to perform actions that should update or modify user data. Then, verify that the data is updated correctly and that no data is lost or corrupted.
Test segregation of duties 	Test the business logic by attempting to perform actions that should be separated among different users. For example, attempt to create a user account and approve the same account from the same user account. Then, verify that the system prevents the user from performing both actions.
Duplicate Registration Check with same email id	Attempt to register a new account with a specific email ID. Then, attempt to register another account using the same email ID.
Overwrite Existing User	Attempt to create a new account with an email address that already exists in the system: First, attempt to create a new account using an email address that already exists in the system.
Weak Registration Implemented	Attempt to register with weak or easily guessable passwords: First, attempt to register with weak or easily guessable passwords such as "password" or "123456".
Password Policy Check	Attempt to create a password that is shorter than the minimum length allowed by the password policy. Verify that the system prevents you from using a password that is too short.
Injection Checks (HTML Inejction/XSS etc) in input parameters	Enter HTML code as input parameters, such as in form fields or search queries, and check whether the application renders the HTML code as expected. If the application renders the HTML code instead of treating it as plain text, it may be vulnerable to HTML injection attacks.
Check for Oauth Misconfiguration	Verify that the OAuth redirect URI is secure and is restricted to a specific domain, and does not allow arbitrary redirections to a malicious site.
User Identity Verification	 Attempt to register with fake or invalid information, such as an invalid email address or phone number, and verify that the system prevents you from creating an account.
